statement.p:
Let S = {\left\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\right\}}\text{.}




How many subsets are there of cardinality 5\text{?}






How many subsets of cardinality 5 have >\{2,3,5\} as a subset?






How many subsets of cardinality 5 contain at least one odd number?






How many subsets of cardinality 5 contain exactly one even number?

webwork-reps.pg:
############################################# ### Generated from PreTeXt source ### ### https://pretextbook.org ### ############################################# ## DBsubject() ## DBchapter() ## DBsection() ## Level() ## KEYWORDS() ## TitleText1() ## EditionText1() ## AuthorText1() ## Section1(not reported) ## Problem1(5) ## Author() ## Institution() ## Language(en-US)
DOCUMENT();
############################################################ # Load Macros ############################################################ loadMacros( "PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "AnswerFormatHelp.pl", "PCCmacros.pl", "contextIntegerFunctions.pl", "PGcourse.pl", ); COMMENT('Authored in PreTeXt');
############################################################ # Header ############################################################ TEXT(beginproblem());
############################################################ # PG Setup Code ############################################################ Context("IntegerFunctions"); $n = random(5,10); #define number of possible elements in set S $S = Set(1..$n); $c = ceil($n/2)+1; #cardinality is equal to 1 more than number of odd numbers in the set. # do {$c = random(4,7);} until ($c < $n) and ($n/2 < $c); #pick a value for subset cardinality $n3 = $n-3; #remaining elements other than {2,3,5} for part b $c3 = $c-3; #remaining elements to be chosen for subset in part b $ans1 = C($n,$c); $ans2 = C($n3,$c3); $ev = int($n/2); $ans3 = C($ev,1);
############################################################ # Body ############################################################
BEGIN_PGML Let [`S = [$S]\text{.}`] a. How many subsets are there of cardinality [`[$c]\text{?}`]
[_]{$ans1}{10}
[@KeyboardInstructions('You can enter C(n,k) for \\(\\binom\{n\}\{k\}\\)')@]**
a. How many subsets of cardinality [`[$c]`] have [`>\{2,3,5\}`] as a subset?
[_]{$ans2}{10}
a. How many subsets of cardinality [`[$c]`] contain at least one odd number?
[_]{$ans1}{10}
a. How many subsets of cardinality [`[$c]`] contain exactly one even number?
[_]{$ans3}{10}
END_PGML
############################################################ # Solution ############################################################
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION a. [`\binom{[$n]}{[$c]} = [$ans1]`] subsets
a. [`\binom{[$n3]}{[$c3]} = [$ans2]`] subsets. We need to select [$c3] of the [$n3] remaining elements of [`S`] to be in the subset.
a. [`\binom{[$n]}{[$c]} = [$ans1]`] subsets. All subsets of cardinality [`[$c]`] must contain at least one odd number.
a. [`\binom{[$ev]}{1} = [$ev]`] subsets. Select 1 of the [$ev] even numbers. The remaining [$c-1] odd numbers of [`S`] must all be in the set.
END_PGML_SOLUTION
############################################################ # End Problem ############################################################
ENDDOCUMENT();
